@*
    A task title as text, with the tags and web addresses typed into it drawn where
    they stand. Every task surface puts its title inside the control that opens or
    renames the task — the row's open button, the card, the panel's rename button —
    so nothing drawn here may be a control of its own: a button cannot hold a link,
    and ARIA makes a button's children presentational, so a nested anchor would
    vanish from the accessibility tree anyway.

    A tag is an inert chip, as it always was on the row. A URL is a span styled as
    a link that a pointer can still follow: a click on it opens the address in the
    browser and stops there, so the row does not open, the panel does not start a
    rename and the card does not open. It takes no tab stop. The keyboard's and a
    screen reader's way to the same address is the real anchor TaskTitleLinks draws
    beside the title, outside the control.

    The two halves of that click live in two places, on purpose. Opening is
    components.js's: a native listener on `data-title-url` calls `window.open`
    inside the click itself, because a browser lets a page open a window only while
    the gesture is still running, and a trip through .NET first — a Blazor Server
    circuit away in the harnesses — can outlast it and be dropped by the popup
    blocker without a word. Stopping is Blazor's: `@onclick:stopPropagation` ends
    Blazor's walk at the span, so the open button's, the card's and the rename
    button's handlers never hear it. A new window is the request every host already
    routes the way it routes an `<a target="_blank">`: a browser opens a tab, the
    desktop head's WebView hands it to the system browser. NavigationManager would
    navigate the harness's own tab away.

    No element of its own, so the host's title element stays the one that ellipses
    and the text inside it is the title, verbatim — see TitleRuns.
*@
@foreach (var run in TitleRuns.Split(Title))
{
    if (run.Kind == TitleRunKind.Url)
    {
        @* data-title-url is the address components.js opens, and what taskListDrag
           keeps out of the row-drag gesture: a press here is a press on the
           address, not on the row. *@
        <span class="task-title-url"
              data-title-url="@run.Text"
              @onclick:stopPropagation="true">@run.Text</span>
    }
    else if (run.Kind == TitleRunKind.Tag && Tags)
    {
        <TagChip Text="@run.Text" CssClass="@TagCssClass" />
    }
    else
    {
        @run.Text
    }
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The title, exactly as stored.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the tags typed into the title are drawn as chips. The row
    /// draws them; the card and the panel list the tags on a line of their own and
    /// leave them as words in the title.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Tags { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Appended to each tag chip's class.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TagCssClass { get; set; }
}
